{
  "markdown_report": "**Synthesis and insights.**\n\nThree starting-point insights guide this early-stage proposal. Training-position margins may describe a different task from appended-mask serving; score agreement must precede interpretation. Broad relevance versus immediate choice is the reported failure, but histories cannot establish ownership, compatibility, or present intent. Missing metadata and empty samples require explicit availability flags, counts, and null statistics.\n\nThree literature insights refine the approach. BERT4Rec’s masked prediction and absolute positions require serving-aligned inputs [1]. History-removal findings motivate temporal sensitivity probes, without establishing inference effects or intent [2]. Candidate sampling changes evaluation behavior, so paired comparisons require identical candidates and eligibility [3,4].\n\n**Directions and framework.**\n\n| Direction | Question | Evidence | Boundary |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Task correctness | Does diagnosis reproduce serving? | Mask checks; exported-score agreement | Engineering validity |\n| Temporal specificity | Which visible positions affect ranking? | Matched deletions; local swaps | Sensitivity, not intent |\n| Measurement coverage | Where are conclusions supported? | Counts; missingness; popularity strata | No invented attributes |\n| Generalization | Do diagnostic hypotheses improve ranking? | Controlled trials; paired validation outcomes | Selection optimism remains |\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality/potential/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n1. **Serving-aligned paired sensitivity probe: 4/7/5.** Originality: integrates useful controls, but overlaps established robustness studies. Potential: supports trustworthy iteration, although sensitivity need not predict HR@10. Difficulty: reuses inference, but fixed-position deletion, predictor parity, and clustered uncertainty require care [1–4].\n2. **Recency-weighted masked-target loss: 3/7/3.** Originality: a practical adaptation, but familiar. Potential: may emphasize recent interests, although older evidence can matter. Difficulty: small loss change, but requires budgeted training comparisons [1,2].\n3. **Popularity-stratified margin audit: 3/6/2.** Originality: applies established stratification, but adds little novelty. Potential: exposes aggregate blind spots, although small strata are noisy. Difficulty: uses training counts, but needs explicit denominators [3,4].\n\n**Selected idea: serving-aligned paired sensitivity diagnostics.** At 0% progress, establish reliable measurements before changing training. Freeze each category’s single BERT4Rec checkpoint. Select deterministic training prefixes and fixed candidates; first construct the exact deployed tensor, including truncation and appended MASK. Within its visible history, delete equal-sized recent or older subsets by masking their attention while retaining surviving position IDs and MASK position. Never backfill. Report compacted deletion separately because it also changes positions. Include deterministic adjacent swaps.\n\nFreeze eligibility against the original complete observed prefix, including deleted and truncated items; future labels never define training-probe eligibility. Report paired target-rank and reciprocal-rank changes, top-10 overlap, coverage, and user-cluster bootstrap intervals. Verify exported scores first; use stable item-ID tie breaking only in diagnostics. Preserve all diagnostic interfaces, measured definitions, unavailable-category lists, and nulls for empty observations.\n\nCap users, prefixes, batches, and runtime; bootstrap cached outcomes on CPU. Stability can reflect insensitivity, while sensitivity can reflect useful recency dependence. Training-prefix results remain exploratory: record all trials, select using unchanged macro validation HR@10, and leave final generalization assessment to the host’s untouched test evaluation [5]. No diagnostic score enters predictions.\n\nReferences: [1] [BERT4Rec](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690); [2] [Betello et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.13165); [3] [Sampling reliability](https://doi.org/10.1145/3705328.3748086); [4] [Krichene and Rendle](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/); [5] [Cawley and Talbot](https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html).",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add serving-aligned, paired history-sensitivity diagnostics to the corrected BERT4Rec baseline, retaining one trained checkpoint per category and all host compatibility interfaces. Control visible context, absolute positions, candidate eligibility, and deterministic ranking before interpreting perturbation effects.",
    "motivation": "Existing training-position margins do not verify appended-mask serving behavior. Qualitative feedback suggests insufficient specificity and recency sensitivity, but supplies no measured evidence that a particular architectural change will improve ranking. Corrected diagnostic controls provide a feasible basis for subsequent experiments; neither ranking stability nor sensitivity is itself an optimization target or evidence of immediate user intent.",
    "implementation_notes": "Preserve DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], all four category routes, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum 200 epochs, and 200 batches per epoch. Keep artifacts beneath the trial directory. Do not change baseline training in this initial proposal, reuse other trials' checkpoints, or fit additional predictors. Each category loads exactly one independently trained BERT4Rec model.\n\nPreserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), view.model, item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), user_train, meta_data, and review_data. Use the actual trained module. Retain the family-specific sample_batch and ModelView contracts; do not infer masked supervision from a shifted next-item loader. Validate positive positions against the supplied sampler's selected-target corruption policy, exclude padding from supervision, and ensure no uncorrupted shifted future target is accessible through bidirectional attention. A sampler using legitimate random-token or unchanged-token corruption branches must be checked against its documented policy rather than incorrectly requiring every supervised token to equal MASK.\n\nUse only training-history prefixes for new probes. For each selected user, choose one prefix deterministically with seed 42: select an endpoint t uniformly from valid endpoints having at least four preceding interactions and an eligible next target; history is interactions before t and target is interaction t. Skip and count targets already present in the complete prefix. Sample 99 unique real-item negatives without replacement from items absent from the complete prefix and distinct from the target; skip and count insufficient candidate pools. These are diagnostic candidates, not the host's validation candidates. Never consult later interactions to construct eligibility. Freeze the complete observed prefix, target, and candidates across variants. Deleted items and items outside the visible window remain seen and excluded.\n\nConstruct the exact original serving tensor first: truncate history to maxlen-1, append reserved MASK, left-pad, and preserve the baseline's actual position IDs. MASK must differ from PAD and all real catalog IDs, remain attention-visible, and never be recommended. Let m be the visible history length and k = min(2, floor((m-1)/2)); skip matched deletions when k < 1. Remove the earliest k or latest k visible interactions by replacing their slots with PAD and excluding those slots from attention. Keep surviving tokens' original position IDs and the original MASK index; never backfill from outside the window. Swap the final two visible history items for the order perturbation. A compacted-and-repadded deletion may be reported separately with its positional changes explicitly identified.\n\nIf baseline log2feats derives positions from nonpadding counts, implement an internal diagnostic forward accepting explicit position IDs and attention masks while preserving the public interface. Verify that its unperturbed output matches log2feats. Interior deletion holes must be excluded from attention keys throughout the encoder. Read the actual appended MASK index, never nonpadding_count-1. Padding-invariance checks must hold logical positions and retained context fixed.\n\nBefore interpreting sensitivity, compare unperturbed diagnostic and exported-predictor scores on identical checkpoint, histories, candidates, evaluation mode, and precision. Report maximum absolute score error, exact ordered-ranking agreement, and top-10 agreement. For float32, predeclare torch.allclose tolerances atol=1e-5 and rtol=1e-4; report failures rather than silently loosening tolerances. Include short, full-length, truncated, and batched-versus-individual examples. Compare logits after changing only external held-out labels; inputs and logits must remain unchanged. Repeated legitimate historical items are allowed. If parity fails, return a diagnostic failure status and withhold substantive sensitivity interpretation.\n\nRank diagnostic candidates by descending score with item ID ascending for ties: sort candidates by ID first, then use descending argsort(stable=True). Do not modify the host evaluator's tie handling. Define target rank as one-based rank, reciprocal rank as 1/rank, and top-10 overlap as intersection size divided by 10. Report variant-minus-original target score, rank, reciprocal rank, and diagnostic hit changes; positive rank change means deterioration. Report exact ordered-ranking agreement separately. These metrics use the fixed diagnostic candidate set and must not be called host validation HR@10.\n\nPredeclare caps of 64 users per category, one prefix per user, ten loader batches for existing margin probes, and 500 CPU bootstrap replicates with seed 42. Sample users uniformly without replacement before inspecting results. Aggregate users equally within category, then categories equally. Resample users independently within each category, retain their paired outcomes and all prefixes if the design is later extended, and recompute the four-category macro average. Report percentile 95% intervals, per-category counts, exclusions, and actual coverage. If any category has no valid observations, report the four-category macro result as unavailable rather than averaging fewer categories. Intervals describe sampled-user uncertainty, not training-seed or candidate-sampling uncertainty. Cache outcomes and bootstrap on CPU. Report elapsed diagnostic time; synchronize CUDA around timed GPU work and account for warmup. All GPU diagnostics consume the shared search budget.\n\nRetain existing embedding-alignment and valid masked-position margin metrics, clearly labeled in-sample. Exclude padding and MASK from embedding sampling; preserve sample seed 42 and cap 2,048 embeddings. A mapped mean cosine near 0.5 denotes approximately zero mean cosine, not demonstrated healthy representation capacity. Return null statistics and zero counts for empty observations. Keep unavailable item-category breakdowns empty with an availability flag. Do not use unavailable ratings, review text, categories, prices, stores, ownership, or simulator traits. Unknown item identity and abstention must not become negative labels.\n\nRestore model training/evaluation state using try/finally. Return metrics and metric_definitions specifying units, directions, denominators, candidate construction, perturbation sizes, tie policy, missingness, and uncertainty. The interpreter prompt must request evidence-linked findings and distinguish serving defects, measured sensitivity, missing information, and hypotheses requiring validation. Record the full search history, including failures. Subsequent recency-loss trials must use the same seeds, splits, compute limits, and pinned evaluator; report paired validation outcomes where available. Repeated validation selection remains optimistic. No diagnostic scores, popularity scores, or independently fitted predictors may be fused into recommendations.",
    "pseudocode": "run_full_diagnosis(train_loader):\n    previous_state = view.model.training\n    try:\n        view.model.eval()\n        with no_grad():\n            verify_reserved_tokens_and_sampler_contract(train_loader, max_batches=10)\n            measure_existing_embedding_and_masked_margin_probes()\n            examples = deterministic_training_prefixes(\n                seed=42, max_users_per_category=64, prefixes_per_user=1)\n            verify_baseline_internal_and_exported_score_parity(examples)\n            if parity_failed:\n                return metrics_and_definitions(status='serving_parity_failed')\n            for example in examples:\n                complete_prefix, target = example\n                candidates = fixed_target_plus_99_unique_unseen_negatives(\n                    complete_prefix, target, seed=42)\n                original = exact_serving_tensor(complete_prefix)\n                # Original includes baseline positions, attention mask, and appended MASK.\n                visible = original.visible_history_indices\n                k = min(2, (len(visible)-1)//2)\n                if k < 1:\n                    record_insufficient_history(); continue\n                recent = delete_slots_as_padding(original, visible[-k:])\n                older = delete_slots_as_padding(original, visible[:k])\n                swapped = swap_tokens(original, visible[-2], visible[-1])\n                # Deletion preserves positions and MASK index; no backfill.\n                for name, tensor in variants(original, recent, older, swapped):\n                    features = compatible_forward(tensor)\n                    scores = view.predict_score(\n                        features[tensor.original_mask_index], candidates)\n                    assert_finite(scores)\n                    ranking = stable_score_order_with_item_id_ties(scores, candidates)\n                    record_scores_ranks_hits_and_top10(name, ranking, target)\n                record_paired_differences_and_overlap()\n            aggregate_users_then_categories()\n            bootstrap_users_within_category_on_cpu(seed=42, replicates=500)\n            return {metrics: measured_results,\n                    metric_definitions: explicit_definitions}\n    finally:\n        view.model.train(previous_state)\n\ndiagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis):\n    embed measured metrics, definitions, counts, and availability flags\n    request evidence-linked findings\n    distinguish serving defects, sensitivity, missingness, and untested hypotheses\n    prohibit inferring intent or validation gains from stability alone",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Combines serving parity, position-preserving interventions, frozen eligibility, and missingness-aware reporting within the required BERT4Rec diagnostic interface.",
      "negative": "History perturbations and robustness diagnostics are established; stronger scientific novelty would require showing that these measurements predict benefits from controlled training interventions."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "Provides a reproducible foundation for subsequent masking, recency-weighting, and representation experiments while preserving evaluation conditions.",
      "negative": "Training-prefix probes cannot establish future-item generalization, and stable rankings can reflect an insensitive model. Their relationship to held-out HR@10 remains unverified."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Reuses one trained model per category, existing scoring interfaces, and cached predictions without additional training.",
      "negative": "Correct interior-hole attention, absolute-position preservation, export parity, deterministic ties, and clustered uncertainty require more engineering than a simple inference wrapper."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "BERT4Rec: Sequential Recommendation with Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformer",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690",
      "contributions": [
        "Introduces bidirectional masked-item modeling for sequential recommendation.",
        "Provides the architectural basis for reserved-mask inference and position-aware diagnostic controls."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish the proposed diagnostic suite or its relationship to this benchmark's HR@10.",
        "Masked-item modeling does not establish equipment compatibility or immediate purchase intent."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Betello et al.: Sequential recommendation history-removal study",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.13165",
      "contributions": [
        "Reports stronger accuracy effects from removing trailing training interactions than beginning or middle interactions.",
        "Motivates position-dependent robustness probes and considers finite rank-biased overlap."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Training-data removal findings do not directly establish fixed-position inference-perturbation effects for BERT4Rec.",
        "Position sensitivity does not demonstrate causal effects on personal intent or guarantee generalization."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sampling reliability in recommendation evaluation, RecSys 2025",
      "link": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3705328.3748086",
      "contributions": [
        "Examines how negative sampling affects evaluation reliability and agreement with full evaluation.",
        "Supports controlling candidate construction during paired comparisons."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not validate training-prefix diagnostics as predictors of the pinned benchmark's validation performance.",
        "Sampled and full-catalog results describe different candidate conditions."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
      "contributions": [
        "Analyzes distortions introduced by sampled recommendation metrics.",
        "Motivates freezing candidate sets when comparing perturbations."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Freezing candidates controls a comparison but does not make sampled metrics equivalent to full-catalog metrics.",
        "Does not justify changing the host's pinned evaluation protocol."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation",
      "link": "https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Shows that model selection can overfit finite evaluation estimates.",
        "Supports recording the complete search history and preserving an untouched final evaluation."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not quantify selection bias for this particular four-category benchmark.",
        "Paired bootstrap intervals do not remove optimism from repeated adaptive validation selection."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch stable argsort documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.argsort.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents stable sorting, enabling reproducible item-ID tie breaking in diagnostic rankings."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "An engineering reference rather than evidence of recommendation-quality improvement.",
        "Diagnostic tie handling must not replace the pinned evaluator's behavior."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch benchmark utilities documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/benchmark_utils.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents warmup and accelerator synchronization considerations for timing."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Timing methodology does not establish the scientific value of a diagnostic.",
        "Actual end-to-end diagnostic cost must still be measured and charged to the search budget."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
